|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_polar_distance_

    Let points $P_1$ and $P_2$ have polar coordinates
    $(r_1, \theta_1)$ and $(r_2, \theta_2)$, respectively:

    |> Image
        src=images/e5_polar_P1P2.svg

    What is the distance from $P_1$ to $P_2$
    as a function of $r_1$, $r_2$, $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$?
    (Keep an eye out for algebraic simplifications and,
    in particular, for the fact that the distance should
    depend only on
    $$
    \theta_1 - \theta_2
    $$
    or, even, only on
    $$
    |\theta_1 - \theta_2|
    $$
    as opposed to depending on the individual values of
    $\theta_1$ and $\theta_2$ separately, since we can rotate
    the entire diagram while keeping the distance from
    $P_1$ to $P_2$ constant.)

    |> Solution

        !! _Solution 1._ As a preliminary, we would like to note that...
        !! _Solution 1._ First note that
        _Solution 1._ As a preliminary, we first note that

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \cos(A - B) = \cos A\cos B + \sin A\sin B
            $$

        for all $A, B \in \rr$, since

        $$
        \cos(A + B) = \cos A\cos B - \sin A\sin B
        $$

        for all $A, B \in \rr$ implies

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        \cos(A - B)\,&=\,\,\up{1.3} \cos(A + (-B))\\
        \            &=\,\,\up{1.4} \cos(A)\cos(-B) - \sin(A)\sin(-B) \\
        \            &=\,\,\up{1.4} \cos A\cos B + \sin A\sin B
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        by $\cos(-x) = \cos(x)$, $\sin(-x) = -\sin(x)$.

        (There is a similar formula

        |> Boxed
            $$
            \sin(A - B) = -\cos A\sin B + \sin A\cos B
            $$
        
        for the sine of a difference, but that one is hardly
        as important or useful, you should forget it even though
        we just boxed it.)

        We now apply the Cartesian formula
        for distance
        
        $$
        \sqrt{(x_1 - x_2)^2 + (y_1 - y_2)^2}
        $$
        
        that will be good to slightly rewrite as

        |> ImageLeft
            src=images/e5_polar_P1P2_a_minus_b_squared_left_cloud.svg

        $$
        \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + y_1^2 + y_2^2 - 2(x_1x_2 + y_1y_2)}
        $$

        !! |> ImageRight
        !!     src=images/e5_polar_P1P2_a_minus_b_squared_cloud.svg
        !!     offset_x=2.5em

        to be applied with $(x_1, y_1) = P_1$, $(x_2, y_2) = P_2$, i.e., with

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        (x_1, y_1) = (r_1\cos\theta_1, r_1\sin\theta_1) \\
        (x_2, y_2) = (r_2\cos\theta_2, r_2\sin\theta_2)\up{1.4}
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        by definition of polar coordinates,
        from which

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        \,&x_1^2 + y_1^2 = r_1^2\cos^2\theta_1 + r_1^2\sin^2\theta_1 = r_1^2(\cos^2\theta_1 + \sin^2\theta_1) = r_1^2,\up{1.3} \\
        &x_2^2 + y_2^2 = r_2^2\cos^2\theta_2 + r_2^2\sin^2\theta_2 = r_2^2(\cos^2\theta_2 + \sin^2\theta_2) = r_2^2\up{1.4}
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        and

        $$
        x_1^2 + x_2^2 + y_1^2 + y_2^2 = r_1^2 + r_2^2
        $$

        (in fact, we know that

        $$
        x_1^2 + y_1^2 = r_1^2
        $$

        and 

        $$
        x_2^2 + y_2^2 = r_2^2
        $$
        
        !! just from the 
        !! Pythagorean theorem
        !! and the fact that $P_1 = (x_1, y_1)$, $P_2 = (x_2, y_2)$, 
        just by the nature of polar coordinates,
        but anyway), while 

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        \,x_1x_2 =\, r_1\cos\theta_1\cdot r_2\cos\theta_2 = r_1r_2\cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2,\up{0.9} \\
        y_1y_2 =\, r_1\sin\theta_1\cdot r_2\sin\theta_2 = r_1r_2\sin\theta_1\sin\theta_2\up{1.5}\,\,
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        from which

        $$
        x_1x_2 + y_1y_2 = r_1r_2(\cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2 + \sin\theta_1\sin\theta_2)
        $$

        but

        $$
        \cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2 + \sin\theta_1\sin\theta_2 = \cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)
        $$

        by the first remark, so

        !! |> Boxed
        $$
        x_1y_1 + x_2y_2 = r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)
        $$

        !! ...after taking this into account,
        !! (we box this formula because
        !! it turns out to be important),
        and, altogether, the distance...
        
        $$
        \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + y_1^2 + y_2^2 - 2(x_1x_2 + y_1y_2)}
        $$

        ...becomes...

        !! |> Boxed
        !!     $$
        !!     \begin{aligned}
        !!     \sqrt{x_1^2 + x_2^2 + y_1^2 + y_2^2 - 2(x_1x_2 + y_1y_2)} \\
        !!     = \sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_2r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}\up{1.4}\,\,\,\,\,\,\,
        !!     \end{aligned}
        !!     $$
        |> Boxed
            $$
            \sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_2r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}
            $$

        ...that, as can be seen, depends only on
        $$
        \theta_1 - \theta_2
        $$
        or, even, only on
        $$
        |\theta_1 - \theta_2|
        $$
        since $\cos(x) = \cos(-x) = \cos(|x|)$ for all $x\in\rr$. 

        |> Pause

        _Solution 2._ Start by rotating $P_1$, $P_2$ by
        $-\theta_2$ radians about the origin, to new
        points $P_1'$ and $P_2'$ such that $P_2'$ is aligned
        with the $x$ axis and $P_1'$ has argument $\theta_1 - \theta_2$:

        |> Image
            src=images/e5_polar_P1P2_rotated.svg

        Since 
        !! $P_1'$ as coordinates

        $$
        \begin{gather}
        \,P_1' \,=\, (x_1, y_1) \,=\, (r_1\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2),\, r_1\sin(\theta_1 - \theta_2)),\\
        P_2' \,=\, (x_2, y_2) \,=\, (r_2, 0)\up{1.8}
        \end{gather}
        $$

        !! while $P_2' = (r_2, 0)$, 
        the Euclidean distance
        formula gives

        $$
        \begin{aligned}
        \overline{P_1P_2}\,=\,\overline{P_1'P_2'}\,
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\sqrt{(r_1\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2) - r_2)^2 + (r_1\sin(\theta_1 - \theta_2) - 0)^2}\\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\sqrt{r_1^2\cos^2(\theta_1 - \theta_2) - 2r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2) + r_2^2 + r_1^2\sin^2(\theta_1 - \theta_2)} \\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\sqrt{r_1^2(\cos^2(\theta_1 - \theta_2) + \sin^2(\theta_1 - \theta_2)) - 2r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2) + r_2^2} \\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\sqrt{r_1^2 - 2r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2) + r_2^2} \\
        =&\,\,\up{1.4}\sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_1r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)}
        \end{aligned}
        $$

        |> ImageRight
            line=1
            src=images/e5_polar_P1P2_euclidean_distance_cloud.svg
            offset_x=-2em

        which agrees with the first solution.

        |> SolutionNote

            As a result, the _distance squared_ between $P_1$ and $P_2$
            is:

            |> Boxed
                $$
                r_1^2 + r_2^2 - 2r_2r_2\cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2)
                $$

            !! (This is maybe easier to remember.)

        |> SolutionNote

            The distance formula reduces to

            |> Boxed
                $$
                \sqrt{r_1^2 + r_2^2}
                $$

                |> ImageRight
                    src=images/e5_polar_P1P2_right_angle_cloud.svg
                    offset_x=3em

            if $P_1$ and $P_2$ are at right angle, since
            $\cos|\theta_1 - \theta_2| = \cos\eta = 0$ in that
            case.
            This accords with the Pythagorean theorem, and
            constitutes an extra check on our computations!

        |> SolutionNote

            By comparing the two solutions one can 
            actually deduce that

            !! |> Boxed

            $$
            \cos(\theta_1 - \theta_2) = \cos\theta_1\cos\theta_2 + \sin\theta_1\sin\theta_2
            $$

            !! if we put ourselves in the shoes of someone who doesn't
            !! already know the angle-sum formulas. 
            !! (Whereas otherwise, if you know the angle-sum
            !! formulas, you use the equation above to simplify the
            !! first solution, as we did.)
            without knowing the angle-sum formula
            for cosine beforehand.
            This constitutes a second* proof of the angle-sum
            formula for cos and, by extension, for sin.

            (\*Some people would say _first_ proof, as our
            original proof from the chapter put some restrictions
            on the range of the angles, such as 
            $0 \leq \theta_1, \theta_2 \leq \eta$
            !! $0 < \theta_1 < \eta$, $0 < \theta_2 < \eta$,
            specifically.) (\*\*Though such restrictions can be lifted
            by diligent of use $\cos(x+\eta) = -\sin(x)$ -type identities.)
